  howpublished = {IEEE Trans. Netw. Sci. Eng.},
  annotation = {中文辅助译名：IEBench：对代理边缘网络中基于 MLLM 的系统的身份事件纠缠概念进行基准测试},
  abstract = {Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have achieved remarkable success in multimodal applications. Their reliance on large-scale, internet-sourced data raises significant concerns regarding security and privacy. These concerns are especially critical when MLLM-based systems are deployed in agentic edge networks, where limited computational resources make retraining impractical for addressing privacy and security issues. Machine Unlearning (MU) has emerged as a critical technique for dealing with these issues. It enables the selective removal of targeted information from pre-trained models without costly retraining. However, the evaluation of MU for MLLMs in agentic edge networks remains inadequate. Existing benchmarks focus narrowly on entities and overlook unlearning of broader visual concepts and their semantic couplings. To bridge this gap, we introduce IEBench. This benchmark enables thorough assessment of MU for MLLM-based systems in agentic edge networks. IEBench features a fictitious dataset of identity entities and corresponding event scenes. It supports the evaluation of unlearning across these distinct yet entangled concepts. We use IEBench to evaluate eight MU methods and reveal their individual strengths and weaknesses. Our findings show that unlearning one concept can unintentionally degrade performance on related concepts within the same image. We term this challenge cross concept interference. Furthermore, we demonstrate the difficulty of unlearning identity},
}

